{
  "pdfpcFormat": 2,
  "disableMarkdown": false,
  "pages": [
    {
      "idx": 0,
      "label": "1",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The FAST 2021 experience paper. Full title: Evolution of Development Priorities in Key-value Stores Serving Large-scale Applications: The RocksDB Experience. This lecture describes its historical setting. \n Source: §1. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Jeff Dean and Sanjay Ghemawat developed LevelDB at Google. Its public open-source announcement was July 27, 2011; this is the release date, not a claim about when work first began. Google's announcement describes a small, portable C++ library and a building block for higher-level storage systems. Bigtable's tablet storage used a precursor. Batched updates across a large key space, including indexes larger than memory, were a design target. Chrome's IndexedDB and Riak's per-node storage were early uses. Source: #link(\"https://opensource.googleblog.com/2011/07/leveldb-fast-persistent-key-value-store.html\")[https://opensource.googleblog.com/2011/07/leveldb-fast-persistent-key-value-store.html] Authors also identified at #link(\"https://github.com/google/leveldb\")[https://github.com/google/leveldb]"
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Facebook created RocksDB from the LevelDB code base in 2012, as stated in the FAST paper. Facebook announced the open-source release on November 21, 2013. The release announcement reports write stalls from single-threaded LevelDB compaction on some server workloads; this is historical evidence, not a claim about every LevelDB workload. RocksDB added parallel compaction, reduced write amplification, and customizable components to use fast SSDs and many CPU cores. Local embedded access also avoided an extra network request for each engine operation. Explain this as a fork with different engineering goals, not a rename or a Google successor release. Release and motivation source: #link(\"https://engineering.fb.com/2013/11/21/core-infra/under-the-hood-building-and-open-sourcing-rocksdb/\")[https://engineering.fb.com/2013/11/21/core-infra/under-the-hood-building-and-open-sourcing-rocksdb/] \n Source: §1, Appendix A. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Created at Facebook in 2012 from LevelDB. MyRocks, TiKV, and stream processors illustrate applications above the library. A shared engine also shares monitoring, debugging, and durability expertise. \n Source: §1, §2.1. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 4–5. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Map abstract LSM components to their concrete implementation. Disk components are sets of immutable files. Tombstones remain until they are safe to discard. \n Source: §2.2. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 5,
      "label": "6",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. A full memtable freezes while a fresh one accepts writes. The WAL records recovery information when enabled. Arrows show logical paths, not precise synchronization or acknowledgment order. Durability settings come later. \n Source: §2.2, §4: WAL treatment. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 6,
      "label": "7",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Conceptual inventory, not exact byte layout. Index entries locate sorted data blocks. Filters and properties help avoid unnecessary work. \n Source: §2.2. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 7,
      "label": "8",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Each flush is a sorted run. L0 file ranges may overlap. Later levels partition the key space into nonoverlapping files within each level. Toy keys and values are illustrative. Width encodes key range, not bytes. Deeper levels are omitted. \n Source: §2.2, Figure 1. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 8,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Get(g) misses memory and finds g=7 in L0, without reading g=2 in L1. Newest visible version wins. Snapshots affect visibility. Filters and caches can avoid physical I/O. Simplified leveled lookup. \n Source: §2.2. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 9,
      "label": "10",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. L0 is special: a-m overlaps h-s, so both L0 files join this input set. Together they overlap all three L1 files. Moving newer overlapping data below older L0 data would break the simplified lookup order. This example makes overlap closure visible. \n Source: §2.2. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 10,
      "label": "11",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The selected files are merged by key and sequence number. The figure shows selected records from the preceding example; other keys are omitted. Sequence numbers and the older k=3 are illustrative. No snapshot needs the overwritten versions. The three output ranges match the preceding L1 ranges. Install the new version before retiring inputs. The memtable k=9 remains outside compaction and newer than disk k=6. \n Source: §2.2. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 11,
      "label": "12",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Introduce why the write behavior of flash motivates LSM storage. The next slides compare an HDD in-place page update with SSD copying, then connect this to LSM batching."
    },
    {
      "idx": 12,
      "label": "13",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Read each cell as an equal-size page. A1 and A2 are the old and new contents of page A. A conventional HDD can overwrite A at the same location without moving B, C, or D. The block here is a conceptual grouping of four pages to align with the next example, not a physical erase unit: HDDs address sectors and do not have NAND's block-erase constraint. Assume full-page aligned writes and omit metadata and drive-specific behavior such as SMR. Background: OSTEP, Flash-based SSDs, section 44.5, #link(\"https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-ssd.pdf\")[https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-ssd.pdf]"
    },
    {
      "idx": 13,
      "label": "14",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. NAND flash programs pages but erases whole blocks. A2 is written to an erased page elsewhere, making A1 obsolete. To reclaim the old block, preserve its live pages B, C, and D by copying them to fresh space, then erase the old block. The figure combines the update and later reclamation, placing A2 and the three copied pages in one fresh block for clarity. An SSD does not immediately copy a whole block for every update, and valid pages need not all go to the same destination block. Here four equal-size page writes inside the SSD divided by one page received from the host gives device write amplification of four. These are illustrative counts, not a measured result; metadata is omitted. Device WA is normally measured over an interval in bytes. Extra writes consume bandwidth and program/erase endurance; wear leveling spreads wear across cells. NAND pages are not necessarily database pages. Source: OSTEP, Flash-based SSDs, sections 44.4, 44.7, and 44.10, #link(\"https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-ssd.pdf\")[https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/file-ssd.pdf]"
    },
    {
      "idx": 14,
      "label": "15",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. A database page and a NAND page are different units; do not equate them. A small in-place record update can cause a database page rewrite before the SSD even sees the write. LSM batching amortizes such small updates, but compaction creates engine-level write amplification. The SSD independently creates device-level amplification through GC and other internal work. Sequential host writes do not guarantee device amplification of one. Endurance is finite because NAND tolerates a limited number of program/erase cycles. Local SSD access also motivated the embedded engine. \n Source: §2.1, §3: write amplification. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 8–11. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 15,
      "label": "16",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Transition to the choice of compaction policy. The next three slides explain leveled, universal/tiered, and FIFO."
    },
    {
      "idx": 16,
      "label": "17",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. In leveled compaction, levels below L0 partition the key space into nonoverlapping files. Pick the L1 g–n file and merge it with L2 files g–k and l–p. Output replaces the selected g–p range; a–f and q–z are untouched. Older versions can be dropped only when snapshot and deletion rules allow it. L0 is special because its files overlap. The earlier compaction input/output slides give the record-level walkthrough. Smaller retained overlap generally helps reads and space but requires more rewriting. Reference: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Compaction\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Compaction] \n Source: §2.2. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 17,
      "label": "18",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. A sorted run may contain one file or several key-partitioned files. Universal is RocksDB's tiered-style policy. This illustrative selection merges the two older runs and leaves the newest alone. Output keeps the latest selected value per key, assuming no snapshot needs older versions: a=5, g=2, m=3, z=6. The newest run still contains g=7, which wins globally. Real selection depends on configured size/run-count/space triggers; it does not always pick exactly two or always pick the oldest. Reference: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Compaction\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Compaction] \n Source: §2.2. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 18,
      "label": "19",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. In the basic FIFO policy, SSTs live in L0. Once total file size exceeds the limit, delete oldest files until it fits. Equal-size files make the example's size limit easy to see. Keys found only in the discarded file are lost intentionally; FIFO is appropriate only when the application permits this retention rule. File age is not a per-key TTL guarantee. Optional merging and TTL variants exist, so do not promise a universal write amplification of one or replace the paper's measured FIFO result. Reference: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/FIFO-compaction-style\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/FIFO-compaction-style] \n Source: §2.2, Table 3. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 19,
      "label": "20",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "4 minutes. Denominators matter: SST bytes include flush and compaction output, excluding WAL and SSD-internal writes. Space amplification 1.095 means 9.5 percent overhead. Tiered uses 12 sorted runs. FIFO uses 20 Bloom bits per key. Direct I/O, cache at 10 percent of fully compacted DB size. FIFO space is N/A because it discards data. Filters make these point reads look similar, while write cost and retained space differ sharply. Physical read I/O depends on access pattern, filters, and cache. Distinguish a point Get from an iterator seek or scan; more read metrics are in the appendix. This experiment is not a prediction for every workload. \n Source: Table 3 and caption, RocksDB 5.9 microbenchmark. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 20,
      "label": "21",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "3 minutes. Answers are visible beside each application. Ask what changes these choices. A write-heavy workload may still choose leveled for read latency or capacity. FIFO requires explicit permission to discard data. These are starting points for the stated constraints, not universal application defaults. \n Source: §1, §2.2, §3. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 21,
      "label": "22",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Introduce Bloom filters from first principles before returning to RocksDB's read path."
    },
    {
      "idx": 22,
      "label": "23",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. A membership query asks whether a key belongs to a set. Here the set is the keys represented by one SST. A Bloom filter is a compact summary made of bits, not another copy of the keys and values. Its negative answer rules out membership; its positive answer requires checking the actual data. Source: Bloom, Space/Time Trade-offs in Hash Coding with Allowable Errors, 1970, #link(\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr05/cos598E/bib/p422-bloom.pdf\")[https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr05/cos598E/bib/p422-bloom.pdf]"
    },
    {
      "idx": 23,
      "label": "24",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Each hash function deterministically maps a key to an array index. This toy filter has eight bits and uses three hash positions per key. Start at all zeros. Insert k at positions 0, 2, 4, then m at 2, 5, 7. Position 2 is shared and stays 1. The final bit vector is 10101101. The hash outputs are chosen for illustration, not computed using a production hash. Source: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/RocksDB-Bloom-Filter\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/RocksDB-Bloom-Filter]"
    },
    {
      "idx": 24,
      "label": "25",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Reuse exactly the same hash functions and bit array. Query g probes positions 1, 3, 5, which contain 0, 0, 1. One zero is enough to reject it. An inserted key would have set all its queried bits, so it cannot be rejected by this intact insertion-only filter. This is the no-false-negatives property. Source: Bloom, 1970, #link(\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr05/cos598E/bib/p422-bloom.pdf\")[https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr05/cos598E/bib/p422-bloom.pdf]"
    },
    {
      "idx": 25,
      "label": "26",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. For k, positions 0, 2, 4 are all 1 and the SST contains k: true positive. For w, positions 0, 5, 7 are also all 1, but only k and m were inserted: false positive. Bits set by different keys can collectively satisfy another query. The actual SST lookup resolves membership, so the filter does not produce a wrong database value. Shared bits also explain why a basic Bloom filter cannot delete a key by simply clearing its bits. Source: Bloom, 1970, #link(\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr05/cos598E/bib/p422-bloom.pdf\")[https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr05/cos598E/bib/p422-bloom.pdf]"
    },
    {
      "idx": 26,
      "label": "27",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Simplified whole-key, full-file filtering for a point Get after memory misses. Among candidate files selected by the read path, a negative filter avoids a data lookup; a positive triggers normal SST lookup and visibility handling. When enabled, filters are built from keys as new SSTs are created by flush or compaction. They live in the SST format and are accessed through memory or cache, so a filter access itself is not always free of I/O. More bits per key generally lower false-positive probability at a memory cost. Prefix filters may also help some seeks; whole-key filters do not remove general range-scan merging. Source: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/RocksDB-Bloom-Filter\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/RocksDB-Bloom-Filter] \n Source: §2.2, §3: CPU utilization, Table 3. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 27,
      "label": "28",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Move from the storage mechanisms to the production measurements that changed RocksDB's optimization priorities."
    },
    {
      "idx": 28,
      "label": "29",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "3 minutes. Table 2 gives one typical use case per category, not fleet averages. Figure 3 separately surveys 42 ZippyDB/MyRocks deployments. Flash endurance means utilization of the endurance budget, not fraction of lifetime already worn out. The cache is an instructive exception. \n Source: Table 2, Figure 3, §3. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 13. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 29,
      "label": "30",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. These objectives accumulate. Write-heavy applications may still prioritize endurance while others prioritize space. \n Source: §3. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 12–20. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 30,
      "label": "31",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Static targets can leave the actual bottom level underfilled relative to upper levels. Dynamic leveling derives targets from the actual last-level size. Under 13 percent here is a measured result, not a universal bound. Random updates, 2 MB/s, DB size about 12–60 GB. \n Source: §3: space amplification, Table 4. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 31,
      "label": "32",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The paper could generally saturate one SSD with a suitable CPU. CPU/DRAM became cost targets even when they did not constrain throughput. Compression and multiple SSDs per CPU can change that. Parallelism is not a claim of lower total CPU work. \n Source: §3: CPU utilization, Appendix A. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 15–20. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 32,
      "label": "33",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The talk compares a CPU with two 2 TB SSDs to a CPU with three. Changing that ratio can exchange unused CPU for unused flash without balancing both. Decoupled storage allows independent provisioning; it does not guarantee full utilization. Longer I/O latency motivates batching and parallelism. Transient failures and QoS also become explicit concerns. \n Source: §3: adapting to newer technologies. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 16–20. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 33,
      "label": "34",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Follow corruption across replicas, memory, files, and storage interfaces, then consider recovery policies."
    },
    {
      "idx": 34,
      "label": "35",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. A checksum is a small deterministic summary of bytes, commonly a CRC for accidental corruption. Save it while data is believed correct and compare against that saved value later. Different bytes can collide, so a match is not a mathematical proof of equality. Checksums detect rather than repair corruption and are not authentication against a malicious modifier. C is a teaching notation. \n Source: §5, Figure 4. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 35,
      "label": "36",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. The RocksDB estimate came from comparing MyRocks primary/secondary indexes, including CPU/memory corruption. Forty percent had propagated. The transfer estimate came from one observed period with storage-stack bugs. These are not universal SSD error rates. \n Source: §5: frequency of silent corruptions. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 30–40. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 36,
      "label": "37",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. A single shard has replicas on different hosts. The application replicates operations using Paxos; RocksDB does not implement that protocol. After unnoticed corruption on host 1, a physical copy builds host 4 and the application retires healthy host 2. Copying 4 to 5 and retiring healthy 3 removes the last good replica. This is unchecked replacement, not a correct repair policy. Early detection instead lets the application discard the bad replica and rebuild from a validated healthy source. CPU/memory corruption can start the same chain even when later file checksums are valid. \n Source: §5: detecting corruption early. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 30–36, 39–40. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 37,
      "label": "38",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Block checksums came from LevelDB. File checksums arrived in 2020. Handoff checksums require a supporting storage API, rarely exposed by local filesystems. Per-KV checksums were in progress. This diagram reports the 2021 design. \n Source: §5, Figure 4. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 29, 37–43. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 38,
      "label": "39",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. The SST checksum is generated when the file is created, saved in its metadata entry, and carried with that identity through transfers. Verify the received contents against that saved expectation. Recomputing a new expected checksum after corruption would merely legitimize bad bytes. Reject a mismatching new replica. Backup and restore both need verification. The 2021 paper says other files, including WAL files, did not yet have this whole-file protection. A complete database copy still includes the necessary metadata and recovery state. \n Source: §5: file integrity, Figure 4. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 37–38. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 39,
      "label": "40",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Block checksums compare with bytes when the checksum was generated. They cannot detect prior memory corruption. A per-KV checksum carried from an earlier boundary can catch corruption before it becomes durable. \n Source: §5: end-to-end protection. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 39–41. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 40,
      "label": "40",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Block checksums compare with bytes when the checksum was generated. They cannot detect prior memory corruption. A per-KV checksum carried from an earlier boundary can catch corruption before it becomes durable. \n Source: §5: end-to-end protection. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 39–41. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 41,
      "label": "41",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. This is the proposed/in-progress design in 2021. Carry a checksum with each record wherever it is copied in memory. Verify writes from the client, memtable flushes, records during compaction, and reads from memtables or block cache before returning them. Existing block checksums protect SST file data, so the per-KV checksum need not be persisted there. Integrity checks do not correct a corrupt record by themselves; recovery still needs a valid source. \n Source: §5: end-to-end protection, Figure 4. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 41. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 42,
      "label": "42",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Distinguish a WAL fragment's internal checksum, storage's own protection, and the checksum across their API boundary. A buffer can be corrupted while being copied, before reliable storage establishes its protection. The WAL checksum might detect this later on a read, too late to reject the acknowledged write. A handoff checksum is computed before copying and verified by the receiver before accepting the write. A suitable API is needed; local file systems rarely provided it in the paper's environment. Remote storage APIs can expose it. \n Source: §5: handoff integrity. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 42–43. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 43,
      "label": "43",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Earlier behavior returned errors or halted writes permanently. Local recovery is preferable for transient conditions. Disk-full needs the condition to clear, not a tight retry loop. Corruption needs isolation and a validated recovery path. \n Source: §5: severity-based error handling. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 44,
      "label": "44",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Introduce the operational demands of many instances, durability choices, file deletion, upgrades, configuration, and replication."
    },
    {
      "idx": 45,
      "label": "45",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Tens or hundreds of shards share a host. Controllers need global budgets and local fairness. Shared pools avoid thread explosion. Separate processes make global coordination harder, requiring conservative allocation or shared usage information. \n Source: §4: resource management. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 22–28. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 46,
      "label": "46",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. WAL records recover writes that have not yet become durable SST data. Replaying the log reconstructs memtable contents after reopening. A WAL segment can be retired only after all data it protects no longer requires it. Sync means forcing log data to persistent storage, assuming the storage stack honors that request. Source: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Write-Ahead-Log-(WAL)\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Write-Ahead-Log-(WAL)] \n Source: §2.2, §4: WAL treatment. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 47,
      "label": "47",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Replication alone does not prove WAL can be disabled. The upper layer must durably preserve and replay acknowledged writes, and coordinate log truncation with persistent engine progress. Buffered WAL durability depends on flush/sync policy. \n Source: §4: WAL treatment. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 48,
      "label": "47",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Replication alone does not prove WAL can be disabled. The upper layer must durably preserve and replay acknowledged writes, and coordinate log truncation with persistent engine progress. Buffered WAL durability depends on flush/sync policy. \n Source: §4: WAL treatment. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 49,
      "label": "48",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. With configurations such as XFS realtime discard, deletion can issue TRIM. SSD translation metadata updates and garbage collection can affect foreground reads. This depends on filesystem settings and firmware. \n Source: §4: rate-limited file deletions. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 50,
      "label": "49",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Define compatibility from the reader's perspective. The paper aimed for at least one year of forward compatibility, with new-feature exceptions. This is the historical policy, not a current blanket guarantee. Unknown optional fields differ from unknown required semantics. \n Source: §4: data format compatibility. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 51,
      "label": "50",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Despite standardization efforts, over 25 configurations appeared across 39 ZippyDB deployments. Default changes can increase memory or concurrency unexpectedly. Some options affect file compatibility. Tools persisted options, validated opening, and supported limited migration. \n Source: §4: managing configurations, Table 5. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 52,
      "label": "51",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Logical copying scans keys without polluting the source cache, then bulk-loads the destination. Physical copying pins a consistent set of necessary files including metadata and recovery state. Filesystem access lets applications reuse copy tools. Copying SSTs alone is not a full replication protocol. \n Source: §4: replication and backup support. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 23–28. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 53,
      "label": "52",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Introduce the limits of local sequence numbers and the motivation for application-defined timestamps."
    },
    {
      "idx": 54,
      "label": "53",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. A sequence number is RocksDB's internal local ordering of writes; it is not an application timestamp or an elapsed-time measure. A user-defined timestamp is supplied by the application and may use a physical or logical clock. Enabling timestamps adds a version field; sequence numbers still exist. There is no automatic universal conversion between sequence numbers and timestamps, and equal sequence numbers on different instances are unrelated. For the same user key, the documented user-timestamp implementation requires seq1 < seq2 to imply ts1 <= ts2. Snapshot visibility and timestamp visibility are both applied on reads. These API details clarify the paper's concepts; timestamp support was partial in 2021. Sources: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/User-defined-Timestamp\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/User-defined-Timestamp] and #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Snapshot\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Snapshot] \n Source: §6: versions and timestamps. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 55,
      "label": "54",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Two retained versions of one user key. The application supplies timestamps 20 and 30, while RocksDB assigns sequences 100 and 101. The application takes and holds snapshot S=100 between the two writes. In the latest view, both writes pass sequence visibility, and the timestamp limit chooses the newest eligible version: T=25 returns 80, T=35 returns 81. With the earlier snapshot, sequence 101 is invisible even at T=35, so the result is 80. This demonstrates that timestamps add a visibility condition rather than replace sequence numbers. Assume simple Put operations with no deletions or merges and both versions retained. Ordinary snapshots must be taken while the desired view is current; they cannot recover discarded history. Source: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/User-defined-Timestamp#dbget\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/User-defined-Timestamp#dbget] and #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Snapshot\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Snapshot] \n Source: §6: versions and timestamps. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 56,
      "label": "55",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "1 minute. Illustrative independent write histories. The same sequence number on two instances does not denote the same application time. The paper's ordinary snapshots are local; this is not a claim that applications can never coordinate snapshots. A distributed protocol must choose a shared read time, ensure relevant writes are applied, and retain the necessary versions. Source: #link(\"https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Snapshot\")[https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Snapshot] \n Source: §6: versions and timestamps. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 57,
      "label": "56",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Timestamp-in-key can require iterators for version queries, and filters are less helpful unless they understand the encoding/prefix. Timestamp-in-value complicates old versions and out-of-order writes. User timestamps are metadata. Transactions were another API extension used by MyRocks. \n Source: §6. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 58,
      "label": "57",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. A conceptual MVCC example, not exact API syntax. Read at 25 selects the newest retained version at most 25. Applications still supply meaningful timestamps and coordinate distributed consistency. File timestamp ranges can prune irrelevant SSTables. \n Source: §6. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 59,
      "label": "58",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Populate then measure the second phase. Baseline encodes timestamps in user keys. These are measured ratios, not guaranteed speedups. Richer semantics improve point lookup, filtering, and file pruning, but cost space, API simplicity, and portability. \n Source: §6, Table 6. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 60,
      "label": "59",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "0 minutes. Bring the resource, durability, and integrity decisions together in a design exercise and recap."
    },
    {
      "idx": 61,
      "label": "60",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Allow one minute for discussion. The following slide expands the answers. Expected answers: host budgets with local caps, durable replay with safe log truncation, pinned consistent file set, version compatibility, and transfer checksums. Compaction policy still depends on capacity and reads. \n Source: Discussion based on §3–§6. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 62,
      "label": "60",
      "overlay": 1,
      "forcedOverlay": true,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Allow one minute for discussion. The following slide expands the answers. Expected answers: host budgets with local caps, durable replay with safe log truncation, pinned consistent file set, version compatibility, and transfer checksums. Compaction policy still depends on capacity and reads. \n Source: Discussion based on §3–§6. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 63,
      "label": "61",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. One possible design, not a unique implementation. Memory examples are budgets rather than measured allocations. Log retention must cover replay of acknowledged writes until engine progress is durable. Pinning preserves the file set; format compatibility allows it to open; checksums test copied bytes. Distributed log catch-up may still be needed after the initial copy. \n Source: Discussion based on §3–§6. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 64,
      "label": "62",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "2 minutes. Students should trace reads/writes, explain compaction tradeoffs, distinguish benchmarks from operational constraints, and justify durability/integrity boundaries. The remaining slides are optional. \n Source: §9, Appendix B. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 65,
      "label": "63",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 0 minutes. Supporting examples, measurements, and project history for reference or further discussion."
    },
    {
      "idx": 66,
      "label": "64",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. Historical framing. Device-specific latency/endurance improvements helped a minority of these workloads. Remote SSDs enabled independent provisioning. This is not a 2026 hardware-market survey. \n Source: §3: adapting to newer technologies. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 67,
      "label": "65",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. KV separation is the WiscKey idea cited by the paper. BlobDB was being added. A reference is cheaper to compact than a large value, but values still need garbage collection and introduce read/locality tradeoffs. \n Source: §3: main data structure revisited. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 68,
      "label": "66",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. Point lookups hide run-count differences when filters are effective. Iterator seeks expose more work. These are benchmark measurements, not asymptotic complexities. \n Source: Table 3 and caption. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong"
    },
    {
      "idx": 69,
      "label": "67",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. Historical milestones as dated in the FAST talk, which includes deployments not listed in the paper's feature timeline. These are original project milestones, not release recommendations. \n Source: Appendix A. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 6. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 70,
      "label": "68",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. Complete the talk's timeline. NVM caching was an experiment. User-defined timestamp support was partial in 2020. These qualifications matter. \n Source: Appendix A. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 6. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 71,
      "label": "69",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 2 minutes. This is the original chart image embedded in the FAST presentation, extracted without redrawing or estimating its bars. The paper describes 42 ZippyDB/MyRocks deployments observed for one month. Each metric is averaged across hosts within a deployment. CPU and read bandwidth use the busiest hour, while space and flash endurance use month averages. Thus do not compare the four metrics as simultaneous instantaneous load. This is distinct from the category examples in Table 2. \n Source: Figure 3 and caption. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 13. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    },
    {
      "idx": 72,
      "label": "70",
      "overlay": 0,
      "forcedOverlay": false,
      "hidden": false,
      "note": "Optional, 1 minute. Preserve the talk's motivating estimates without treating them as the RocksDB Table 3 benchmark. The talk characterizes engine-level write amplification as often in the hundreds, file systems as near one, and SSD internal amplification as one to three. The paper separately describes software amplification up to about 100 and SSD amplification of 1.1 to 3. These differ in framing and rounding. Layer factors multiply only when adjacent byte-count boundaries align; adding these factors would be wrong. \n Source: §3: write amplification. https://www.usenix.org/conference/fast21/presentation/dong \n Talk pages 9–10. https://www.usenix.org/sites/default/files/conference/protected-files/fast21_slides_dong.pdf"
    }
  ]
}
